This problem can be easily solved by finding a pattern in $\mathbf{A}^{n}$ and generalize to $\mathbf{A}^{100}.$
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^{2} &= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} 
&= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} 
&= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}^{-1} 
\end{align*}
Thus $\mathbf{A}$ is a double reflection matrix, reversing the vector components along both diagonal directions. (Schematically, this means that if we were to draw a "x" over the diagonals, the path of every point would start and end with a "x.") Therefore $\mathbf{A}^{100}=\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.$
